        "title": "Problem 3: Encapsulated Employee Wage Calculator with Overtime",
        "difficulty": "Easy",
        "problemStatement": "Build an `EmployeeWage` class with `private String employeeName`, `private double hourlyRate`, and `private int hoursWorked`. Enforce minimum wage invariant: `hourlyRate` cannot be set below $15.00/hr (clamp to 15.0 if lower). `hoursWorked` cannot be negative. Provide method `logHours(int hours)` that increases `hoursWorked` if hours > 0. Provide method `calculateWeeklyPay()` which pays regular rate for the first 40 hours, and 1.5x overtime rate for any hours above 40. In `main()`, create an employee with $20.00/hr, log 45 hours, and print their weekly pay.",
        "hint": "Overtime hours = Math.max(0, hoursWorked - 40). Regular hours = Math.min(40, hoursWorked).",
        "solutionCode": "public class Solution {\n    static class EmployeeWage {\n        private String employeeName;\n        private double hourlyRate;\n        private int hoursWorked;\n\n        public EmployeeWage(String name, double rate) {\n            this.employeeName = name;\n            this.hourlyRate = Math.max(15.0, rate);\n            this.hoursWorked = 0;\n        }\n\n        public boolean logHours(int hours) {\n            if (hours > 0) {\n                this.hoursWorked += hours;\n                return true;\n            }\n            return false;\n        }\n\n        public double calculateWeeklyPay() {\n            int regularHours = Math.min(40, hoursWorked);\n            int overtimeHours = Math.max(0, hoursWorked - 40);\n            return (regularHours * hourlyRate) + (overtimeHours * hourlyRate * 1.5);\n        }\n\n        public String getEmployeeName() { return employeeName; }\n        public double getHourlyRate() { return hourlyRate; }\n        public int getHoursWorked() { return hoursWorked; }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        EmployeeWage emp = new EmployeeWage(\"Alex Vance\", 20.0);\n        emp.logHours(45);\n        System.out.println(\"Employee: \" + emp.getEmployeeName());\n        System.out.println(\"Hours Worked: \" + emp.getHoursWorked());\n        System.out.printf(\"Total Pay: $%.2f%n\", emp.calculateWeeklyPay());\n    }\n}",
        "output": "Employee: Alex Vance\nHours Worked: 45\nTotal Pay: $950.00",
        "explanation": "Calculated properties like weekly pay should be computed on-demand via methods rather than stored as public mutable fields."
